From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 07:01:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 19/19] kboot: admit the J604 on the T8132 ISP handoff path

aurora-silicon/aura #84 (76742bfd, ported in the two commits before
this one) hands the camera firmware over only on the J713. Gate it
through the T8132 board table instead, as every other T8132 handoff
path is, with a new T8132_PATH_ISP that the J713 and the J604 take.

What the path reads, J604 against J713 :
- the isp, ispRtb and dart-isp nodes match byte for byte in the 25G83
  and 26B123 templates apart from phandles and sensor-type: 0x2e on the
  J604, 0x17 in the J713 isp node. sensor-type becomes
  apple,platform-id, as on the J713;
- the J604's runtime segment-ranges under the 25G83 boot firmware
  (four runtime ADTs read on the J604, 2026-11-05) are __TEXT 0xca4000 at
  0x100_0000_0000 and __DATA 0x748000 right above it, inside DRAM and
  the kernel's 20 MiB window. They are the values the J713 test uses.
  The macOS 27.2 iBoot's __DATA is 0xe1c000 and would be refused,
  leaving the camera disabled, which is the designed failure mode.

The log lines now name the SoC (T8132 ISP) instead of the J713. The
J713 behaviour is unchanged; its test now builds the board table in.
test_j604_isp_handoff.py runs the production code on a J604 tree with
the J604's values and adds a T8132 board that is not admitted (J773g),
which is left alone. It fails without this commit.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
